More Vulnerabilities That Can Be Paid (821–830)
821. Improper Validation of API Batch Requests
Batch Request Authorization Bypass
Attackers execute multiple operations in a batch request without proper permission checks.
💰 Typical payout: $400 – $8,000
822. Insecure Handling of Mobile Push Notification Tokens
Push Notification Token Exposure
Push notification tokens are leaked or reused, allowing attackers to send fake notifications.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $6,000
823. Improper Validation of File Preview Access
File Preview Authorization Bypass
Users can preview files they should not have permission to access.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $6,000
824. Insecure Handling of Password Reset Notifications
Password Reset Notification Abuse
Attackers trigger excessive password reset emails to disrupt users.
💰 Typical payout: $200 – $4,000
825. Improper Validation of User Status APIs
User Status Manipulation
Attackers modify online/offline status or activity data of other users.
💰 Typical payout: $200 – $4,000
826. Insecure Handling of Application Telemetry APIs
Telemetry Data Exposure
Telemetry APIs expose internal application metrics or usage data.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $6,000
827. Improper Validation of Organization Settings APIs
Organization Settings Authorization Bypass
Attackers modify organization-level settings without proper privileges.
💰 Typical payout: $500 – $10,000
828. Insecure Handling of API Gateway Logs
API Gateway Log Exposure
Logs from API gateways expose sensitive requests or authentication tokens.
💰 Typical payout: $300 – $6,000
829. Improper Validation of Account Privacy Settings
Privacy Settings Authorization Bypass
Attackers change privacy settings belonging to other users.
💰 Typical payout: $200 – $4,000
830. Insecure Handling of Server Monitoring Dashboards
Monitoring Dashboard Exposure
Public monitoring dashboards reveal server health and infrastructure details.
💰 Typical payout: $400 – $8,000
✅ High-impact vulnerabilities that usually pay the most on platforms like HackerOne and Bugcrowd include:
Remote Code Execution
Server-Side Request Forgery
SQL Injection
Broken Access Control
Cross-Site Scripting
Tools often used by security researchers include Burp Suite, Nmap, OWASP ZAP, and FFUF.
